
.idea/
*.iml
*.iws

# Claude Code's working directory. Two separate reasons, and the second is the sharp one:
# settings.local.json is per-machine preference, and .claude/worktrees/ holds entire checkouts of
# this repository. Committing that nests a copy of the repo inside itself.
.claude/


out/
build/

core/db/database.properties
core/src/main/resources/db/database.properties
core/src/main/resources/application.yaml
# The *test* config is committed on purpose — it holds no secrets, and CI starting the context is
# what keeps it in step with the ${easy.*} placeholders in main. Only a local override is ignored.
core/src/test/resources/application-local.yaml

web/.env.local

# Minted fresh on every fake-idp run; see core/dev-idp/README.md
core/dev-idp/tokens.json

aae/gunicorn-conf.py

# Runtime artifacts
*.log
*.dylib
.gradle/

# Kotlin 2.x build metadata
.kotlin/

# Python bytecode (aae/)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# aae's virtualenv. A venv writes its own .gitignore containing `*`, so this is belt and braces —
# but the day someone builds one with a tool that does not, it is a few thousand files in a diff.
.venv/
aae/.pytest_cache/

# Rotated logback archives next to the app. The rotation pattern is easy.log.2026-07.gz, which
# neither *.log nor *.log.gz matches — the date sits between the .log and the extension.
*.log.*

# Dev host security posture — deliberately not in a public repo.
#
# Not because any of it is a secret in the Kerckhoffs sense (key-only auth, ufw and fail2ban are
# standard practice and saying so weakens nothing), but because this document is specific: which
# accounts can log in, that keys are the only credential in existence, that there is no console
# rescue path, and what to do when locked out. That is a reconnaissance packet for one named host.
# The reusable *reasoning* lives in ansible/roles/hardening/ instead.
doc/dev-hardening.md

# The real inventories: host addresses and, more to the point, the complete list of accounts that can
# log in. Each environment's `hosts.example.yml` is the committed template. Roles and per-environment
# group_vars stay in the repo — they are the reusable half, and the reason prod's eventual rebuild is
# a known quantity.
ansible/inventories/*/hosts.yml

# Production's group_vars, unlike dev's, which stay in the repo as the worked example of what an
# environment loosens and why. Production's are values rather than examples: the names its
# certificate covers, the Moodle endpoints it writes grades into, the monitoring server's address,
# the confidential Keycloak client core authenticates as, and which port a service with no
# authentication of its own listens on and who may reach it.
#
# None of it is a credential. All of it describes how to approach three named internet-facing
# machines, and this repo is public — the same reason doc/dev-hardening.md is excluded above.
# `inventories/production/hosts.example.yml` says what belongs in these files.
ansible/inventories/production/group_vars/

# The production update runbook: the three addresses, the accounts that can log in, OS and Keycloak
# versions, disk headroom, and a full description of an unauthenticated service and its port. It is
# the most useful single document an attacker could be handed about these hosts, and it exists to be
# worked from rather than published. Ask kspar.
doc/production-update.md

# Production's deploy environment, for the same reason as its group_vars above: the SSH target, the
# release branch it is allowed to run, and the hostnames. `deploy/dev/` stays in the repo — dev is
# the environment a fresh checkout is meant to be able to deploy to, and production is not.
#
# Worth being precise about what is and is not secret here: `config.json` is served to every browser
# that loads the site, so its contents are public by design and ignoring it protects nothing. It is
# ignored anyway, so that the rule is "production's deploy directory is not in git" rather than a
# per-file judgement somebody has to re-make. `deploy/README.md` says what belongs in it.
deploy/prod/

# Playwright output, if a run is ever started from the repo root rather than web/. web/.gitignore
# covers the normal case; this catches the stray one, which is otherwise a committed artifact
# nobody notices.
test-results/
playwright-report/
